{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: days 1–15 forecasts are generally too high vs. realized (≈ +0.5 to +2 €/t). Apply a small downward shift early in the path.",
    "Late-horizon underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days of history show an upswing, the model underestimates days ~20–30 (misses trend extension; realized can be +3 to +7 €/t above forecast). Add an upward trend-extension term to the back end when momentum is positive.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs in history, the model projects levels that stay elevated too long; realized prices mean-revert faster (notably around mid-horizon ~10–20). Damp forecast amplitude after sharp run-ups (reduce projected levels and/or increase reversion speed).",
    "Excessive dip depth around mid-horizon: the model often forecasts deeper pullbacks (mid-path troughs) than realized by ~0.5–1.5 €/t. Clip/dampen negative deviations from the recent mean during days ~12–20.",
    "Volatility misallocation: the model is too smooth early but too conservative late—realized volatility and upside tail increase toward days 25–30 in these samples. Inflate late-horizon variance and allow higher upside drift when regime is bullish."
  ]
}
